well-known fellow,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.530
by the name of Ilya Grigorik.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.120
We're going to put a blog
post out on the Google Cloud

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.764
Platform blog.

00:01:24.764 --> 00:01:25.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.640
MARK MANDEL: All about HTTP/2
and what Google Cloud Platform

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.870
does to enable HTTP/2.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.540
You can read the
full article, but I

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.623
think the really big
takeaway which is really cool

00:01:34.623 --> 00:01:36.934
is basically, we kind
of just do it for you.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:37.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.970
MARK MANDEL: So really,
if you want HTTP/2,

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.130
put an HTTPS load
balancer on it,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.200
and that's pretty
much all you need.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.404
As long as it goes through
HTTPS, we do HTTP/2.

00:01:47.404 --> 00:01:49.820
If you're that bingeing customer
and you go through HTTPS,

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.346
you get HTTP/2.

00:01:51.346 --> 00:01:52.870
So you just get a
speed boost, which

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.220
is awesome, without really
having to do much work.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.136
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:01:56.136 --> 00:01:58.960
And for those that don't
know what HTTP/2 is-- I mean,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.790
HTTP/2-- yeah, the
version two of HTTP.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:01.620
MARK MANDEL: It's better.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.536
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sure.

00:02:02.536 --> 00:02:04.200
But why?

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.590
Why is it better?

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.910
MARK MANDEL: So that is
an excellent question.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.785
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I know
that one of the thing

00:02:09.785 --> 00:02:13.670
is it's way faster because it's
able to basically-- the server,

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.720
rather than having to cache on
the client every single time,

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.390
the server is able
to predict what

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.432
they're going to ask for
and just send you stuff.

00:02:20.432 --> 00:02:22.014
MARK MANDEL: So it
can do pre-loading,

00:02:22.014 --> 00:02:23.200
which is pretty interesting.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.790
So we can send stuff in advance.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.780
It tends to use a
single connection so you

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.320
can do streaming a lot better.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.903
So where we used to
do tricks before--

00:02:29.903 --> 00:02:32.306
there used to be one
where you'd chart it

00:02:32.306 --> 00:02:34.930
across different domains and do
all those sort of fancy tricks.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:35.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.380
MARK MANDEL: You have to
worry about that sort of stuff

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.210
as much anymore too.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:37.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:38.850
MARK MANDEL: It does
really neat stuff.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.470
It's well worth
looking at the spec.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.902
It's not particularly long.

00:02:41.902 --> 00:02:42.860
It's pretty neat stuff.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I also
heard one of the things

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.720
that I liked the least
from web development, which

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.357
is when you have to put all the
images into one single image.

00:02:50.357 --> 00:02:52.190
MARK MANDEL: Oh, you're
doing image spreads.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:52.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.040
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which
is so painful to do.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.230
Basically, it's
not needed anymore.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it makes
things a whole lot easier.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:02:58.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it's really cool.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:00.285
And I really like
that-- the fact

00:03:00.285 --> 00:03:03.000
that you can basically
forget about HTTP/2.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.740
Just know that it's going
to be there for you.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.074
You just need to download
balancer before and that's it.

00:03:08.074 --> 00:03:09.323
MARK MANDEL: It's pretty cool.

00:03:09.323 --> 00:03:11.659
It's actually really cool
in that we sort of have

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:14.200
experimentations in there for
quick support on some platforms

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:14.360
too.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:14.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.343
MARK MANDEL: Which
is also very cool--

00:03:16.343 --> 00:03:18.090
which is a UDP streaming
type of protocol

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.670
that some browsers support.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:20.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
[INAUDIBLE] is kind of magic.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:21.240
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:21.757
It's magic.

00:03:21.757 --> 00:03:22.840
I don't know how it works.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I just
makes everything faster.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:24.930
I don't really know how.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.434
MARK MANDEL: It's special magic.

00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:27.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.320
Cool.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.905
So yeah, that's a cool
thing of the week.

