00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:42.050
you can contact us.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.480
MARK MANDEL: Brilliant.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:44.313
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:03:44.313 --> 00:03:47.390
So for the main content--
compute as a continuum.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.390
So this is really interesting.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:53.000
So actually you had a really
good metaphor for this.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.927
I really like it.

00:03:54.927 --> 00:03:56.010
I actually really like it.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.720
I kind of want to start there
with the metaphor you had.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:03:59.553
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:03:59.553 --> 00:04:03.110
So basically-- well,
for the computer options

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.030
we have-- basically
you can say you

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.162
have the Infrastructure
as a Service or IaaS,

00:04:08.162 --> 00:04:09.370
or whatever you pronounce it.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.090
I'm very bad at
pronouncing that.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:15.501
You have also platform as
a service-- PAAS or Paas.

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:17.209
And then there is
something in the middle

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:18.230
we'll discuss about later.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:18.890
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.348
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And I was thinking

00:04:20.348 --> 00:04:22.110
how to make the
difference between both.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.430
And well, I'm European,
and in Europe,

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.396
other than being just
in general better,

00:04:27.396 --> 00:04:31.030
we also have manual cars.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.030
MARK MANDEL: We have those
in Australia as well.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:33.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, but you drive

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:34.972
on the wrong side of the road.

00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:37.180
MARK MANDEL: In America,
they call them stick shifts.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:37.468
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, wow.

00:04:37.468 --> 00:04:37.540
OK.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:38.248
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:04:38.248 --> 00:04:41.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we
have stick shifts then.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.420
And I was thinking about
the difference between cars

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.252
and I asked-- driving a
manual car or a stick shift

00:04:48.252 --> 00:04:49.460
and driving an automatic car.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.680
But then I realized that
actually that's kind of wrong.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.835
It could be more like when
you have Infrastructure

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:57.020
as a Service, you're
driving a manual car.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.770
When you have PaaS,
it's basically

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.230
you are in a self-driving car.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:02.980
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.580
I actually really,
really like this.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.297
Because Infrastructure as a
Service is really like-- its

00:05:07.297 --> 00:05:08.130
your infrastructure.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.640
It's like you're
spinning up VMs.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.960
You get just basic
machines, nothing on them.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.990
It's totally up to you how
you wanted to do stuff.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:16.740
If their stuff goes
down, it's on you--

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:17.500
all that sort of stuff.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.958
Sort of like being
in a manual car,

00:05:18.958 --> 00:05:21.350
if you want to change
gears, you have to do it.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:21.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.589
MARK MANDEL: If you want
to go faster or slower,

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:24.887
you have to break.

00:05:24.887 --> 00:05:27.470
If you've been given a car, then
it's up to you to go forward.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:29.940
And at the same time, you could
totally mess up your gear box

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:30.720
if you do it wrong.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:31.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:31.850
MARK MANDEL: And that's on you.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:32.400
That's totally on you.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.483
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I may
have done that before, yes.

00:05:34.483 --> 00:05:36.970
MARK MANDEL: I may have
done similar things.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.350
But then, I really like
the idea as-- platform

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.207
is a service, where
you say to the car,

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:42.790
like if you've got
a self-driving car,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.470
you're like, hey, I
want to go to the shops.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:45.380
Take me to the shops.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.900
And the car decides the
route you want to take.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.640
It decides how to
get there, how fast

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.900
to drive, avoiding obstacles.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.900
And so on platform as a service,
you don't have as much control,

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.504
but it's like, well, now I can
just get to where I need to go

00:05:57.504 --> 00:05:59.670
and I don't have to do a
whole bunch of other stuff,

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.270
such as infrastructure
tasks or security as much,

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:03.270
or things like that.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.974
So I actually really
like that metaphor.

00:06:04.974 --> 00:06:07.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And actually
following the metaphor

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.580
a little bit
deeper, you can also

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.190
have the thing
that is like, when

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.910
you need to learn how to drive
a self-driving car, well,

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:13.580
that's it.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.121
You just get in the self-driving
car and it's just like drive

00:06:16.121 --> 00:06:17.950
me to the store That's it.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:20.700
Learning how to drive
a stick shift-- well,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.010
that's a little bit more time.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.551
So it's going to
[INAUDIBLE] a while.

00:06:23.551 --> 00:06:26.150
Like when you are
a platform service,

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.390
everything you need to know
is what you want to do.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:28.890
That's it.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:29.280
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: When you have
infrastructure as a service,

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.780
you're going to have
to decide on what

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:34.520
OS you want to
run, what libraries

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.850
you are going to be using, how
do you secure your network.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.100
MARK MANDEL: How do you log
it, how do you monitor it,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.420
make sure it stays up-- yeah,
all that sort of fun stuff.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah, I
really like that metaphor.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:44.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:45.553
I think that worked really well.

00:06:45.553 --> 00:06:47.430
I really like it.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.859
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So how
do we offer those things

00:06:49.859 --> 00:06:50.900
in Google Cloud Platform?

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.810
MARK MANDEL: So
Infrastructure as a Service--

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.930
we have Google Compute Engine.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:58.750
So we can fire up
virtual machines.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.320
You have a variety of operating
systems-- so Debian, Ubuntu.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.900
We even offer Windows if
that's your flavor of choice--

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.680
and a few others as well.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.144
We have a list online which
shows you which ones of ours

00:07:10.144 --> 00:07:11.560
are supported and
how much server.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.289
We do core OS.

00:07:12.289 --> 00:07:13.330
We do all sorts of stuff.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.250
But they are-- they
are basic machines.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:18.410
We put some stuff in them to
make them a little more secure,

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.902
and ability to SSH into them
really easily, but that's it.

00:07:20.902 --> 00:07:21.610
They're machines.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:23.360
You can do what you like.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.300
Any extra details I
might have missed?

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.466
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Not really.

00:07:26.466 --> 00:07:29.220
It's basically, you just
get virtual machines

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:30.770
and you do whatever
you want to them.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.990
So you can do SSH,
do apt-get install.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.500
You can mess around.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.690
You can break your machine
down basically, and then

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.360
just delete it and create
a new one if you want to.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.330
MARK MANDEL: Probably
most interesting--

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.534
you can do up to 32 cores,
we can do SSD disks.

00:07:44.534 --> 00:07:45.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.600
And there's all the
networking parts, load

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.650
balancers as we mentioned
before and so on.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:54.500
So there's basically-- if
you walk into a data center,

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.040
like an ideal data
center, where you can just

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.109
say I want more and
more machine here,

00:07:59.109 --> 00:08:01.400
and now I want to connect
them with this cable-- that's

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:02.250
basically what you do.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:03.260
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yeah.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:06.035
And you can usually provisioning
tools-- Ansible, Chef, Puppet,

00:08:06.035 --> 00:08:07.160
all that sort of fun stuff.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:07.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.010
There's also the monitoring
that comes directly from it,

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.730
so you can see how often
the machine has been used.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:13.520
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.603
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's
a lot of things going on,

00:08:15.603 --> 00:08:19.850
but it's basically just basic
components of virtual machines

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:20.591
that you manage.

00:08:20.591 --> 00:08:21.090
Yeah.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.360
MARK MANDEL: We do make
some things a bit easier.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:24.400
Our monitoring service kind
of hooks in automatically

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:25.250
with the machines.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:27.625
So that's one thing you don't
have to necessarily manage.

00:08:27.625 --> 00:08:30.370
But, yeah, that's
Infrastructure as a Service.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.220
That's compute engine.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.500
You can spin up VMs.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:36.262
So all the way on the other
side, Platform as a Service--

00:08:36.262 --> 00:08:37.220
what have we got there?

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:38.928
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
Platform as a Service

00:08:38.928 --> 00:08:40.370
is one of my favorite products.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.122
I use it all the time.
it's Google App Engine.

00:08:43.122 --> 00:08:43.830
MARK MANDEL: Woo!

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:45.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And what
Google App Engine does

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:48.080
is basically this idea
of a self-running car.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.980
You write your code, you just
put it there, and that's it.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.910
You don't need to
manage anything else.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:59.150
You don't know
how many instances

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.174
are running-- just
like enough instances

00:09:02.174 --> 00:09:03.590
to run the traffic
you're getting.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:04.530
That's it.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.700
So it's really easy
to get started,

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:07.989
but also it scales very well.

00:09:07.989 --> 00:09:09.780
We talked about it a
little bit last night.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:10.550
MARK MANDEL: On the
previous episode.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:12.883
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, because
the Google Cloud Platform

00:09:12.883 --> 00:09:14.710
podcast is actually
hosted on App Engine.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:16.810
But it's so simple to use.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:21.090
You have huge, huge players
like Rovio with Angry Birds.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:22.960
They use that and it
just works very well.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.860
So basically, the good
thing is that you're

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.010
giving a little
bit of flexibility

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:32.220
that you have with
Infrastructure as a Service.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.180
So you cannot run
everything you want.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:38.680
You have like a series
of allowed runtime.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.130
So you have Go, of course.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:44.832
You have Java, you
have Python, and PHP.

00:09:44.832 --> 00:09:45.540
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And then
that's pretty much it.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:51.025
There are some limitations on
how you can access the network

00:09:51.025 --> 00:09:51.900
and things like that.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:55.620
But it's kind of easy to
adapt any application to it,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.990
but you still have to adapt it.

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:57.960
MARK MANDEL: It's
worth noting though

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.330
that you can't run your
own custom code on there.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.429
You can't write to
the file system.

00:10:02.429 --> 00:10:04.220
You have to go through
some of our storage.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:05.944
Requests can take
longer than 60 seconds.

00:10:05.944 --> 00:10:06.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.180
MARK MANDEL: Stuff
like that-- and that's

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:09.630
kind of the way it's built.

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:10.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.716
The limitations-- for
instance, the fact

00:10:11.716 --> 00:10:15.800
that you can write
to the file system,

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:18.130
it's actually a very
good best practice.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:20.517
Because if you try to do
that on a single machine

00:10:20.517 --> 00:10:22.600
and the machine goes down,
you just lost the data.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:22.940
MARK MANDEL: Sure.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:24.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
for a web server,

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.460
it's actually a good practice.

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:27.690
So basically, we're
trying to enforce the best

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.630
practices for web development.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:33.140
So then once you're doing
that, we can make sure

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:35.040
that your code will
scale to whatever traffic

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:35.490
you're getting.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:35.730
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.250
It means we can do things that
make things easier internally

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.000
to be out of scale and
do stuff like that.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.500
But much like your
self-driving car,

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:43.210
you can't be like,
oh, actually we

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:45.600
need to go faster or
take this shortcut

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.320
or something like that, for
very specific instances.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:51.220
Like no, the self-driving car is
going to get you there the way

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:52.090
it's going to get you there.

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:52.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:53.470
That's it.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:58.270
And I think most of the time
it's basically what you want.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.230
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:00.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
For me, by default,

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:03.400
I will develop stuff
for App Engine.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.180
And if I get to some point
where I want to run something

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.800
like FFmpeg or Image
Magic, or things

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.730
like that, that won't
run on App Engine,

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.820
then I will spawn a
compute engine instance

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.409
on the infrastructure
service side.

00:11:15.409 --> 00:11:17.950
And you can have both of them
make them communicate together.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.826
So yeah, you can get
the best of both worlds.

00:11:20.826 --> 00:11:22.200
MARK MANDEL: Best
of both worlds.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.180
So speaking of best of
both worlds-- fantastic

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.980
segue-- we were talking a
little bit before about how

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.740
we can have stuff that sort of
sits in between Infrastructure

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.420
as a Service and
Platform as a Service.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:34.128
And I think we have
a couple of offerings

00:11:34.128 --> 00:11:35.500
that they're available there.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
so that's the good thing

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:40.690
that the word continuum
implies indeed.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:44.530
Yeah, so we have both ends--
Infrastructure as a Service

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.970
on one side, Platform as
a Service on the other.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:48.720
What's in between?

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:50.511
MARK MANDEL: So we have
a couple of things.

00:11:50.511 --> 00:11:53.200
We just talked about App Engine,
and falling under that banner,

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.240
we have something that's
called Manage VMs.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:55.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:56.129
That's cool.

00:11:56.129 --> 00:11:59.220
MARK MANDEL: Which I think
is actually really cool.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:04.760
Manage VMs pretty much lets you
to sit in the App Engine world

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.644
where we manage things
for you, but allows you

00:12:07.644 --> 00:12:10.060
to-- I hate to use the term,
but it actually makes sense--

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.830
like break the glass ceiling.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.220
We had to break through
and say, OK, I actually

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.470
want to be able to either
run my own run times

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:19.830
or write my own
code the way I want

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.510
to write it, and take
longer than 60 seconds--

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:23.700
things like that.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:26.540
But I'm willing to give
up a little bit in terms

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:29.074
of either the management side.

00:12:29.074 --> 00:12:29.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.730
MARK MANDEL: So
pretty much we've

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:34.044
got Ruby support on there.

00:12:34.044 --> 00:12:35.210
I actually have to remember.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.297
Node.js.

00:12:36.297 --> 00:12:38.630
A few other ones I can't think
of off the top of my head

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.740
right now.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.220
And you can also provide your
own Docker files as well.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.170
So you can bring your own
custom run times if you want,

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:46.480
and this way you can do that.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.313
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
the good thing about it

00:12:48.313 --> 00:12:50.390
is that we've also been
working on the fact

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:54.050
that now you can
deploy to manage VMs

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:56.750
without having to install
Docker on your own machines.

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:58.840
So basically, you
just give the code

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:00.530
and we'll build
it and deploy it.

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:04.090
So the developer experience is
quite similar to App Engine.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:04.920
It's pretty good.

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.460
Just write your
code and send it.

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:08.585
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and it's
meant to be very close.

00:13:08.585 --> 00:13:11.715
So you can also still write
Java and Python, and PDP and Go,

00:13:11.715 --> 00:13:12.840
much like you would before.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:13.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:14.980
MARK MANDEL: So if you have
an existing App Engine app,

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:15.620
and you're like, you know what?

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:17.402
For this particular
bit of my code,

00:13:17.402 --> 00:13:18.610
I actually need to break out.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.443
Maybe I need FFmpeg
like you were mentioning

00:13:20.443 --> 00:13:22.100
before-- things like that.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:23.650
I can still have
the infrastructure

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:25.850
that I'm used to
with App Engine,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:28.208
but I can go that little
bit further and be like,

00:13:28.208 --> 00:13:30.666
oh, I have a container, so I
can put whatever I like in it.

00:13:30.666 --> 00:13:32.320
And that's really
powerful stuff.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:33.170
I really like it.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.628
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So yeah, basically

00:13:34.628 --> 00:13:36.595
that's just going a little
bit in the direction

00:13:36.595 --> 00:13:39.309
of Infrastructure as a Service
but definitely next to Platform

00:13:39.309 --> 00:13:39.850
as a Service.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:40.380
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.504
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's very
Platform as a Service A.

00:13:42.504 --> 00:13:44.424
MARK MANDEL: A, yeah.

00:13:44.424 --> 00:13:46.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do we have
something in the middle?

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:47.750
And the answer is,
of course, yes.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:48.400
MARK MANDEL: Of course we do.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.440
We have something, I think,
a little bit further more

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.731
towards the Infrastructure
as a Service side, but still

00:13:52.731 --> 00:13:54.574
in that Platform as a Service.

00:13:54.574 --> 00:13:55.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:58.994
That's Kubernetes,
which, I love the name.

00:13:58.994 --> 00:14:01.160
MARK MANDEL: So I have a
tendency to say Kubernetes.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:02.930
I think it's just
because I'm Australian.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:03.430
I don't know why.

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:05.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I mean,
it's a Greek word anyway.

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:07.330
So I don't think that either
of us is pronouncing it.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:08.454
MARK MANDEL: You know what?

00:14:08.454 --> 00:14:09.920
Somebody should
definitely send us

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:11.200
a phonetic sounding--
someone who

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:12.410
has a Greek background--
I think that sounds great.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:13.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh
yeah, that would be nice.

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:14.330
MARK MANDEL: We'll play it.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:15.480
I think we should play it.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.320
So Kubernetes is an
open source project that

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:19.768
was first started by Google.

00:14:19.768 --> 00:14:21.726
We could talk about this
for an entire episode.

00:14:21.726 --> 00:14:22.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:23.900
MARK MANDEL: I think
we'll get some people on.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:25.820
I've got some interviews
definitely lined up.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:28.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That could
be a nice episode too, yeah.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:30.440
MARK MANDEL: But what we
have is a hosted version

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:32.630
called Google Container
Engine, which is essentially

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:34.460
hosted Kubernetes.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.302
Kubernetes is
really, really cool.

00:14:36.302 --> 00:14:37.760
The way I like to
explain it really

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:40.560
quickly is basically-- so
it's really a container

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.030
orchestration service.

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:42.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:44.266
MARK MANDEL: And the way
I like to think of it

00:14:44.266 --> 00:14:45.439
is-- containers are great.

00:14:45.439 --> 00:14:46.980
We could go on with
them for a while.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:50.626
But what do you do when you're
like, I need five of these type

00:14:50.626 --> 00:14:52.750
and 10 of these type, and
I need six of these type.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.374
I need to make sure this one can
talk to that one, and this one

00:14:55.374 --> 00:14:57.500
talks to that one,
and these two always

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:00.010
get started up together
and that sort of stuff.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:01.240
That's what Kubernetes does.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:03.865
So it's a scheduling service to
make sure that, hey, yeah, I've

00:15:03.865 --> 00:15:06.180
got five running of this,
I've got six running of that,

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:07.630
I've got ten running of these.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:11.594
And if the machine blows up or
a docker container goes down,

00:15:11.594 --> 00:15:13.760
then it starts it back up
again, and says, oh, wait.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:14.468
That's gone down.

00:15:14.468 --> 00:15:15.070
Sweet.

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:17.050
And what's cool about
it, which I quite like,

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.250
is sort of the
ubiquity of containers.

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.490
It doesn't really matter
what's running inside them.

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:22.239
It doesn't matter.

00:15:22.239 --> 00:15:25.204
Kubernetes can just do
it in a very standard way

00:15:25.204 --> 00:15:27.620
for all sorts of different
types of software applications.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:28.495
So I kind of like it.

00:15:28.495 --> 00:15:30.411
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
and just to give a more

00:15:30.411 --> 00:15:33.010
concrete example-- something
that I have seen, for instance,

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:36.320
is you have the typical--
the head of world

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:38.390
of the [INAUDIBLE]
existence, which is basically

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:41.670
the guestbook-- the
database that moves around.

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:43.520
I really like that
example because basically

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:46.840
what you're saying is, well,
you have the web servers.

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:49.560
And then you also
have a database.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:51.660
When you have those
web servers, maybe you

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:53.690
want to scale-- the
web server container,

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:54.890
you want to scale it over.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:58.280
And [INAUDIBLE] is like, oh, I
had one container doing that.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.500
Now I want to have 10 of those.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:05.320
And maybe have five hosts- five
actual machines running that.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:08.060
And you don't want those ten
containers running, all of them

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.240
in one single machine.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:12.000
So you want to say, oh, yeah,
just [INAUDIBLE] across.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.250
And that's what
Kubernetes will do.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:16.540
If one of those five
machines goes down,

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:18.870
those two containers will
just move somewhere else.

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:19.530
That's it.

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:21.860
So basically it's
the idea of you

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:24.250
describe what you
want to have running.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.330
That's what we call the
declarative approach, where

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:30.270
you don't say what to do
when something goes wrong.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:32.100
You just say this
is what I want.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.630
Do everything you
need to make that.

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:34.380
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:37.680
So you still have the
ability to drive the car,

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.030
but there is some
self-driving aspects to it.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:42.680
It is really in that
middle, isn't it?

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:42.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:42.950 --> 00:16:44.385
I kind of like to
think about it--

00:16:44.385 --> 00:16:47.210
you're basically a self-driving
car, where you're basically

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:48.850
saying you know what?

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.580
I actually want to go
through these streets

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:53.880
and I need to avoid that part.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:58.284
So it's more like very,
detailed self-driving.

00:16:58.284 --> 00:16:59.825
MARK MANDEL: Sort
of like lane assist

00:16:59.825 --> 00:17:03.014
or self-parking or
something like that.

00:17:03.014 --> 00:17:03.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:06.150
MARK MANDEL: Just to
go into that metaphor.

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:07.849
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, at some
point that metaphor will break,

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:08.349
I'm sure.

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:12.520
So that's Kubernetes.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.650
And Kubeternetes--
basically what it does

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:18.060
is it's just that--
orchestrate containers

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:19.880
across a fleet of machines.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:23.000
Those machines can be running
on Google Cloud Platform,

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.981
on Google Container Engine.

00:17:25.981 --> 00:17:27.730
They could also be
running somewhere else.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.180
They could be running
on your own premises.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:32.580
They don't be running
anywhere else.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:35.110
What Google Container
Engine is, is

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:39.552
just Kubernetes-- the whole
thing is managed by one master.

00:17:39.552 --> 00:17:41.010
So what we're doing
with Kubernetes

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:45.200
is giving you an easy way to
spawn those instances that you

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.310
need to run the containers, and
then one extra instance, which

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:51.500
is what we call a Kubernetes
Master, that is managed by us.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:53.709
So we make sure that that
machine will never go down.

00:17:53.709 --> 00:17:55.541
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that's the important one.

00:17:55.541 --> 00:17:56.320
It's pretty easy.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:57.153
It's pretty awesome.

00:17:57.153 --> 00:17:59.446
You can go to the Developer
console, click a button.

00:17:59.446 --> 00:18:00.570
How many nodes do you want?

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:01.980
I'd like five, please.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.495
You click OK, and
it spins it all up

00:18:03.495 --> 00:18:05.370
and creates it for you,
which is pretty cool.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:05.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:06.950
It's a very, good experience.

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:07.700
I really like it.

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:09.629
MARK MANDEL: I really,
really like it too.

00:18:09.629 --> 00:18:11.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
should definitely

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:13.020
do an episode on Kubernetes,
because there's really

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:13.540
cool things.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:14.520
MARK MANDEL: There's
so many cool things.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:15.019
OK.

00:18:15.019 --> 00:18:18.610
So to recap, I guess-- so
on one side of the spectrum,

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:19.860
we have Platform as a Service.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.220
So we've got App Engine.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:22.790
And then as we
continue a little bit

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:24.623
further towards
Infrastructure as a Service,

00:18:24.623 --> 00:18:25.500
we have Manage VMs.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:25.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.160
MARK MANDEL: We continue
a little bit further

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:28.480
towards Infrastructure
as a Service.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:29.870
So we've got Container Engine.

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:30.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And Kubernetes.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:31.680
MARK MANDEL: And Kubernetes.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.300
And then we go all the way
over on the other side--

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:35.570
Infrastructure as a Service--
we've got Compute engine.

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:35.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:37.850
MARK MANDEL: So that's
the continuum really.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:38.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's it, yeah.

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:40.240
MARK MANDEL: That
seems pretty cool.

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:41.961
So really, whatever
you want to build,

00:18:41.961 --> 00:18:43.335
we should have a
solution for you

00:18:43.335 --> 00:18:45.170
and hopefully we can help you
along the way as well with

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:46.090
some of our tools.

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:46.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:48.970
I think that basically,
