00:19:11.247 --> 00:19:12.580
we have a questions of the week.

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:13.330
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:15.850
We have a question of the week.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:18.740
So this seemed to
be around someone's

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:22.290
trying to SSH into their
machine from their laptop--

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.030
from like a terminal command.

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:27.320
And they're like-- this
is an interesting one.

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:29.340
So they know their
details are correct.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.700
So I'm sitting at my machine
and I know my password.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:33.950
And my credentials are
all set up correctly.

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:36.860
But every time I SSH in, it
keeps dropping my connection

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:38.800
and I'm really confused
about what's going on.

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:39.716
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:39.716 --> 00:19:41.670
So that happened to me once.

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:42.480
MARK MANDEL: Yeah?

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.021
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
was quite painful

00:19:44.021 --> 00:19:46.740
because-- if you know
Linux enough, probably

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:48.600
you know exactly
what is the answer.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:50.999
I'm not a Linux expert
so it got me confused

00:19:50.999 --> 00:19:52.040
a little bit for a while.

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:54.090
MARK MANDEL: So I like to
think I'm a Linux expert.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:55.650
I have been using
Linux for a long time,

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:57.730
but I actually didn't know
the answer to this at all.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.015
So I thought this was
actually really interesting.

00:19:59.015 --> 00:19:59.931
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:59.931 --> 00:20:02.910
So basically if you
tried-- every single time

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:07.120
you tried to SSH or actually
to connect in any way

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:10.250
to a Compute Engine
instance, there's

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:11.930
going to be a log
going on somewhere.

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:14.660
We're going to--
just in case we want

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:16.520
to measure-- if someone
is trying to attack

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:17.340
you-- these kind of things.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:17.860
MARK MANDEL: Security stuff.

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:18.480
Good stuff.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:19.396
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:19.396 --> 00:20:21.900
So if you're running
Ubuntu, by default,

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:24.126
you have this little
program called Sshguard.

00:20:24.126 --> 00:20:26.000
I'm not sure if I'm
pronouncing it correctly.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:27.127
MARK MANDEL:
Sshguard-- I think that

00:20:27.127 --> 00:20:28.660
sounds-- between the two of
us, I'm sure it will be fine.

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.826
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
Ssguard-- something like that.

00:20:30.826 --> 00:20:33.440
And what Sshguard does is
every single time there's

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:37.870
a connection event-- so as
someone is searching into it--

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:41.089
we'll measure the dangerousness
of the connection.

00:20:41.089 --> 00:20:42.630
MARK MANDEL: So like
how risky it is.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:43.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
How risky it is.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:44.890
MARK MANDEL: It seems
a little bit strange.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.806
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:45.806 --> 00:20:49.440
And the things that when you're
connecting from your laptop--

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:54.510
it turns out that very often
there's some DNS settings going

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:58.250
on, and basically your laptop
appears as something slightly

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:00.380
risky for that instance.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.030
It doesn't mean it's
completely risky,

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:03.844
but it doesn't really trust it.

00:21:03.844 --> 00:21:04.510
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:06.426
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So it
gives the dangerousness

00:21:06.426 --> 00:21:09.100
of 10, which is a completely
random number to choose.

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:12.470
But the thing is that
once you get over 40,

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:13.880
then you get banned.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.820
So that's why you may be
able to get it once, twice,

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:17.350
three times.

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:19.820
And the fourth time your
connection is dropped.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.500
And the only error message
you get is your connection

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:23.279
is dropped.

00:21:23.279 --> 00:21:25.320
MARK MANDEL: So it's not
like you're reconnecting

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:26.810
a lot over a short period.

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:27.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Basically, yeah.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:28.810
MARK MANDEL: After a
while, it goes down.

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:30.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: After
a while, it goes down.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:30.480
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
what's the solution for that?

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:34.469
Well, the first
thing you need to do

00:21:34.469 --> 00:21:38.070
is you need to make sure that
that's actually the problem.

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:40.890
Maybe your network is not broken
and you're not able to connect.

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:42.710
So that could be.

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:45.900
So how do you connect when
you cannot SSH into a machine?

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:51.110
Well, what is going on is that
your IP has been banned, right?

00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:54.570
But you can still connect
using the console.

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:56.880
From the console, you
have the SSH button.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:58.474
That will work
every single time.

00:21:58.474 --> 00:21:59.140
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:01.359
So Google Cloud Platform
knows for compute and SSH,

00:22:01.359 --> 00:22:02.900
if it's doing it
through the browser,

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:04.400
it's like oh, that's special.

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:05.410
We know that's great.

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:07.659
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We know who
you are when you're using

00:22:07.659 --> 00:22:09.110
a private key identification.

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:09.900
So it's different.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:11.760
MARK MANDEL: So you're probably
logged in with two-factor auth

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:13.090
because we're all using
two-factor auth when

00:22:13.090 --> 00:22:14.200
we use our Google accounts.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:15.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
all should, yeah.

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:17.250
MARK MANDEL: So we trust
that connection a lot.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
so that's the thing.

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:19.820
That will always work.

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:21.460
Then once you
connect in there, you

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:26.500
can do IP tables dash capital
L, so pseudo IP tables,

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:28.150
depending on who you are.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:31.047
And that will list
all the rules.

00:22:31.047 --> 00:22:33.380
Probably you're going to find
one of those little things

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:35.110
that your IP has been banned.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:38.827
You can just drop it, so just IP
tables dash help or something.

00:22:38.827 --> 00:22:39.910
You'll see how to drop it.

00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:42.584
It's quite simple, and once
you're dropped it, that's it.

00:22:42.584 --> 00:22:43.250
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:44.730
Now you're allowed
back in again.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:47.365
Can you white list yourself and
be like, OK, am I always OK?

00:22:47.365 --> 00:22:48.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:49.620
You can white list yourself.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.980
Depending on the
instances and the rules

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:53.610
that you have on
your connection,

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:55.180
it may be harder or easier.

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:58.440
One of the things that you can
do is just say this is my IP.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:01.404
Don't ever block this
IP, and then you're good.

00:23:01.404 --> 00:23:02.070
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:02.860
That sounds great.

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:03.140
All right.

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:03.679
Cool.

00:23:03.679 --> 00:23:04.220
